  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAside from a few stray showers that could develop across\nportions of north central and northeast Ohio later this\nafternoon into early evening, dry conditions expected until\nshower chances increase through the day on Saturday. A\npositively tilted upper trough/closed low and attendant surface\nlow pressure will slowly move east from the mid Mississippi \nvalley into the Ohio valley Saturday through Sunday. \n\nShower and storm chances increase late tonight into early \nSaturday morning from west to east. The best chances for precip \nduring the daytime will generally be west of the I-71 corridor, \nparticularity in the far southwest portion of the forecast area\nfrom Findlay to Marion. Precip chances will increase across the\nrest of the area Saturday night as the low level jet ramps up \nand convergence increases across the area. Heavy rainfall will \nbe possible with any convection, as PWAT values increase to \n1.75-2.00 inches across the area. Hi-res guidance seems to focus\nthe heaviest precip west of the area on the northwest flank of \nthe surface low, but this precip maxima could sneak into the \nI-75 corridor by early Sunday morning depending on the evolution\nof the system. WPC is highlighting the far western part of the \narea in a slight risk of excessive rainfall Saturday and \nSaturday night.\n\nAs the upper trough amplifies over the region Sunday, mid/upper\nflow will increase a bit on the downstream side of the trough.\nShower and storm activity will impact the forecast area as the\nsurface low slowly pivots eastward. WPC continues to highlight a \nslight risk of excessive rainfall for much of the forecast area\non Sunday with the combination of slow moving/training storms \nand intense rainfall rates leading to an increase in flash flood\npotential. SPC is highlighting areas east of the I-77 corridor \nwith a marginal risk of severe weather. Deep layer effective \nshear of 30-35 kts during the afternoon, combined with MLCAPE \nvalues of 1000-1500 j/kg, could lead to some strong to severe \nthunderstorms as the convection tracks east out of the area into\nthe evening. Damaging winds would be possible, and a tornado or\ntwo cannot be ruled out with low LCLs and somewhat favorable \nhodographs. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConfidence increasing in unsettled weather across the area\nMonday through Wednesday, as aforementioned mid/upper trough\ncuts off over the Ohio valley Monday. This will bring diurnally\ndriven shower/storm chances through Wednesday before the low\nfills and lifts northeast into New England ahead of a northern\nstream shortwave moving into the northern Great Lakes region.\nThis will also keep conditions humid and temperatures near\nseasonal normals. Areas that see heavy rainfall over the weekend\ncould be vulnerable to an increased risk of flash flooding with\nany slow moving or training storms during this period.",
